<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Armstrong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Louis Daniel Armstrong, Satchmo, Satch, Pops</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Armstrong was an American jazz trumpeter, cornetist and singer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, among the most influential figures in jazz, who shifted the music's focus from collective improvisation to solo performance. Raised in poverty, he followed his mentor King Oliver to Chicago in 1922, and his Hot Five and Hot Seven recordings from 1925 showcased his solos. He was one of the first African-American entertainers to reach wide white and international audiences, toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Asia for the US State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a>, and spoke out publicly during the Little Rock school desegregation crisis of 1957. His recording of Hello, Dolly! reached No. 1 in 1964. He died in Queens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, in 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-armstrong.jpg" alt="Louis Armstrong" width="330" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harry Warnecke / Gus Schoenbaechler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Armstrong_in_Color_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In music Duke Ellington, <b>Louis Armstrong</b> and Fletcher Henderson led bands, and Eubie Blake, Noble Sissle, Jelly Roll Morton, James P. Johnson, Fats Waller, Ethel Waters and Florence Mills performed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Golden Gate Quartet recorded it in 1957, <b>Louis Armstrong</b> for the album Louis and the Good Book in 1958, and the Robert Shaw Chorale in 1964, with liner notes by Langston Hughes.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li><li><blockquote>It has since been recorded by Mahalia Jackson, <b>Louis Armstrong</b>, Lena Horne, Paul Robeson, Harry James, Sam Cooke and many others; in 1962 Georgia Lee, the first Indigenous Australian artist to record blues songs, included it on her album.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nobody-knows-the-trouble-ive-seen">Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen</a></li><li><blockquote>Bessie Smith's 1925 recording, with <b>Louis Armstrong</b> on cornet, entered the Grammy Hall of Fame in 1993, and Smith sang it on screen in the 1929 film St. Louis Blues; the 1929 recording by Louis Armstrong &amp; His Orchestra, with Red Allen, joined it in 2008, and Handy's…</blockquote><a href="/songs/saint-louis-blues">Saint Louis Blues</a></li></ul></section>
